            <ArticleTitle>Revising common beliefs in the management of stable CAD.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>65-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/nrcardio.2012.184</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>New findings published in 2012 have challenged common beliefs about stable coronary artery disease. Studies have shown that β-blockers and n-3-polyunsaturated fatty acids have no impact on prognosis, that percutaneous coronary intervention is not always the best option, and that women do not have worse cardiovascular outcomes than men.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
